          {
            "id": "/mycc.md/architecture-core-concepts",
            "text": "**AgentContext** — the modular state container and central interface through which tools interact with the system. Modules: `core` (work dir, logging, user questions), `todo`, `mail` (async inter-agent mailbox), `team`, `issue`, `skill`, `bg`, `wt` (git worktree), `wiki`. Two implementations: `ParentContext` (main, direct access) and `ChildContext` (child, IPC wrappers).\n\n**Triologue** — the conversation state manager: tracks message history, manages `microCompact()`/`autoCompact()` for token management, tracks the `Sequence` for hookish skill conditions, supports hint round generation for confusion recovery. Since the 2026-08 layered refactor, `src/loop/triologue.ts` is a thin **facade** over sibling modules in `src/loop/triologue/`: `store.ts` (MessageStore — message array, token accounting, project-context populators, defensive hole filtering), `pending-tools.ts` (PendingToolLedger — pending tool-call resolution + alignment validation), `compact.ts` (transcript save, summary/focus prompts, `runAutoCompact` via a `CompactDeps` adapter), `wrap-up.ts` (WrapUpManager — wrapUpMark lifecycle), `checkpoint.ts` (pure checkpoint query functions), and `types.ts` (shared types incl. `TriologueInternals` — the narrow interface tp-auto-fixer consumes instead of the full class; public API shrank by deleting dead `getMessagesFrom` and moving the five `_`-prefixed internals behind `TriologueInternals`).\n\n**AgentIO Singleton** (`src/loop/agent-io.ts`) — manages I/O state: the LineEditor (prompt input), ESC/neglected mode, process type detection. Key: `isMainProcess()` (true in lead, false in child/teammate), `isNeglectedMode()`/`setNeglectedMode(value)`, `onNeglected(callback)`, `ask(prompt)` (main-only), `exec(cmd)` (main-only), `execEditor(args)`. Bang mode: typing `!` at prompt start switches to `run cmd ! ` (`BANG_PROMPT`). **Child processes cannot use `ask()`/`exec()` — they throw.** Use `ctx.core.question()` (IPC) for user questions and `ctx.core.brief()` for logging.\n\n**IPC and IOC** — IPC enables main↔child communication via Node.js `child_process.fork()` and message passing. IOC: `TeamManager` acts as a dispatcher — modules register handlers for specific message types (Open-Closed Principle). Key: `IpcRegistry` (dispatcher), `sendRequest`/`sendNotification` (primitives), Request-Response pattern for operations requiring results.\n\n**Loader** — unified loading of tools and skills with hot-reload for dynamic content. Priority (highest wins): (1) built-in (`src/tools/`, `skills/`), (2) project (`.mycc/tools/`, `.mycc/skills/`), (3) user (`~/.mycc-store/`). Methods: `loadAll()`, `watchDirectories()` (hot-reload), `getToolsForScope(scope)`.\n\n**State Machine** — the agent loop uses explicit states instead of a single `while(true)`: **prompt** (get user input, handle slash/bang/exit), **collect** (pre-LLM pipeline: questions, mail, hint round, todo nudge), **llm** (build system prompt, call LLM with retry), **hook** (augment tool calls, evaluate hook conditions), **tool** (execute tool calls sequentially), **stop** (handle no-tool-call case, await teammates).\n\n**Sequence** — a wrapper around Triologue providing query methods for hookish skill conditions to inspect conversation history (`seq.has('edit_file')`, `seq.count('bash')`, `seq.since('git_commit')`).\n\n**Confusion Index** — a metric quantifying how \"stuck\" an agent is, triggering hint rounds. Scoring: `+1` per assistant response, `+0` for exploration tools, `-1` for action tools, `+2` for tool errors, `+1` for repetition. When score >= threshold (default 10): main triggers hint round (LLM self-analysis); child sends mail to lead requesting guidance.\n\n**mindmap** — a tree-structured knowledge system compiling markdown files (like `MYCC.md`) into a navigable JSON structure. Each node has an ID (slash-separated path), text, title, summary (LLM-generated), level, children, links. The agent queries nodes via `get_node`/`recall` for efficient context retrieval. Compilation is one-way: `MYCC.md --> [compile_mindmap] --> mindmap.json`. Each agent has an independent mindmap instance (process isolation). **Patch line**: agent-discovered knowledge is recorded as append-only patches in `.mycc/mindmap-patch.jsonl` (add/update/delete), replayed into the in-memory tree at load time. `/mindmap compile` rebuilds patches (BFS dedup/stale-removal) and **preserves MYCC.md-deleted nodes as `add` patches** so trimmed content survives as patch-added knowledge. See `docs/mindmap-design.md` and `docs/mindmap-redesign.md`.\n\n**wiki, vector store** — a persistent knowledge base using LanceDB for vector similarity search, with WAL (Write-Ahead Log) files for audit and rebuild. Each document has hash, domain, title, content, references, embedding vector. Components (`src/context/parent/wiki.ts`): `wiki.get/put/rebuild/listDomains`. Tools: `wiki_get`, `wiki_put`, `wiki_prepare`. Config: embedding model `nomic-embed-text` (`OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODEL`), duplicate threshold 0.95, content limits 50-1000 chars. WAL stored in `~/.mycc-store/wiki/logs/YYYY-MM-DD.wal`; rebuild via `/wiki rebuild`.\n\n**Auto-Claim** — idle child processes automatically claim unassigned issues. When a child enters IDLE state (no tool calls), it polls for new mail and scans issues for `pending` + no owner + no blockers, then atomically claims the first match.\n\n**health-check** — runs at startup to validate Ollama connectivity and model availability: (1) server reachable, (2) model exists and can process requests, (3) `TOKEN_THRESHOLD` ≤ 80% of model's context length. Implementation `src/setup/ollama-health-check.ts` (`checkHealth` → `HealthCheckResult`). CLI flag `--skip-healthcheck` bypasses; on failure, prompts retry or exit.",
            "title": "Architecture & Core Concepts",
            "summary": "Architecture centers on **AgentContext** (src/context/parent-context.ts): a modular state container with modules core/todo/mail/team/issue/skill/bg/wt/wiki/peer; `ParentContext` gives direct access and registers all IPC handlers (issue/team/wiki request-response), while `ChildContext` wraps them in IPC calls. **Triologue** (src/loop/triologue.ts) is, after the 2026-08 layered refactor, a thin facade over src/loop/triologue/: `store.ts` (MessageStore: message array, token accounting, project-context populators, defensive hole filtering), `pending-tools.ts` (PendingToolLedger for tool-call resolution/alignment), `compact.ts` (transcript save + `runAutoCompact` via a `CompactDeps` adapter), `wrap-up.ts` (WrapUpManager ESC lifecycle), `checkpoint.ts`, and `tp-fix.ts` (TpAutoFixer injects bridge messages on role-transition violations; ollama/deepseek allow tool→user natively). The five `_`-prefixed internals and `TriologueInternals` interface were deleted — recovery now gets a private `TpFixContext` adapter. **AgentIO** (src/loop/agent-io.ts) is a singleton for I/O: `isMainProcess()`, neglected mode + `onNeglected` callbacks, `ask()`/`exec()` main-only (children use ctx.core.question/brief via IPC), bang mode, and an ask() re-entrancy queue. The **state machine** (src/loop/state-machine.ts) uses explicit states PROMPT/SLASH/COLLECT/LLM/HOOK/TOOL/STOP/WAIT with TurnVars (per conversational turn) and PassVars (per COLLECT pass) tiers. IPC uses IoC via **IpcRegistry** dispatch. Other systems: hot-reloading Loader (built-in/project/user priority), **Sequence** history queries for hook conditions, **Confusion Index** triggering hint rounds, LanceDB-backed wiki with WAL, mindmap with append-only patch replay, auto-claim of pending issues by idle children, and startup Ollama health-check.",
            "level": 2,
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "/mycc.md/architecture-core-concepts/hookish-skills",
                "text": "Skills that actively trigger based on patterns in the conversation sequence (vs. passive skills loaded on explicit request). Defined with a `when:` field in YAML frontmatter. Condition language queries conversation history using two scope prefixes: `turn.*` (current turn, since last user query) and `session.*` (entire livelog, since session start or last compact). Functions: `turn.count(tool?)`, `turn.lastIndex(tool)`, `turn.countResult(tool, pattern, maxChars?)`, `turn.hadError(tool?)`, `session.count(tool?)`, `session.lastIndex(tool)`, `session.countResult(tool, pattern, maxChars?)`, `session.hadError(tool?)`, plus global `isPlanMode()`. Tool spec (three-class): plain tool (`\"toolName\"`), skill_load (`\"skill_load#skillName\"`), bash (`\"bash#commandPrefix\"` — clause-split by `;`/`&&`/`||`, then prefix match). Call context: `call.metadata.*` (filePath, newLoc, existingLoc, isDestructive), `call.args.*` (raw tool args). Actions: `inject_before`, `inject_after`, `block`, `replace`, `message`, `compact`.\n\n**Condition Compiler Principles** — translates natural-language `when` into structured `{ trigger, condition, action }` via a safety-first pipeline: (1) **Lazy Compilation** — conditions are NOT compiled eagerly; a skill with `when` is \"pending\" until `skill_compile` is invoked (pending hooks are injected into LLM context at startup). (2) **LLM Translation with Structured Output** — `when` is sent to the LLM with all tools and a JSON schema enforcing the output shape. (3) **Expression Safety via jsep AST** — conditions are parsed with jsep and walked recursively (NOT `eval`/`new Function`); only `seq`/`call` root identifiers, only known `seq.*` functions, no dangerous identifiers, only safe string/array methods on results. (4) **Retry with Error Feedback** — up to 3 retries. (5) **Smoke Test** — evaluate against an empty mock sequence before persisting. (6) **Atomic Persistence** — temp file + rename, with backup. (7) **Source File Tracking & Orphan Cleanup** — `sourceFile` as `\"{layer}:{path}\"`; orphans auto-removed. (8) **Version History** — every compilation creates a new version in the `history` array (full audit trail). Key files: `src/hook/conditions.ts`, `src/hook/condition-validator.ts`, `src/hook/evaluator.ts`, `src/tools/skill_compile.ts`.",
                "title": "Hookish Skills",
                "summary": "Hookish Skills actively trigger based on conversation patterns via a `when:` field in YAML frontmatter (vs. passive skills). The condition language queries history with `turn.*` (current turn, cleared at markPromptBoundary) and `session.*` (entire livelog, cleared on compact) scopes — count, lastIndex, countResult(pattern, maxChars?), hadError — plus global `isPlanMode()` and `call.metadata.*`/`call.args.*` context. Tool specs are three-class: plain name, `skill_load#name`, `bash#prefix` (clause-split by `;`/`&&`/`||` then prefix match). Actions: inject_before/after, block, replace, message, compact.\n\nCompilation is lazy: pending skills (with `when`, no condition) are injected into LLM context until `skill_compile` runs. ConditionRegistry.compile() uses LLM structured output (JSON schema) with up to 3 retries carrying error feedback; compileCondition() enforces validation gates — jsep AST walking (no eval/Function; only turn/session/call/isPlanMode roots, blocked dangerous identifiers, whitelisted string/array methods, legacy `seq.X` rejected) and a smoke test against an empty mock sequence — before atomic persistence (temp+rename, backup, rollback), orphan cleanup via `sourceFile`, and version history. bash inject/replace args require intent language (`VERB OBJECT TO PURPOSE`). Legacy conditions surface for recompile prompting.\n\nRuntime: HookExecutor evaluates conditions per batch with priority ordering (compact/block=0 > replace=1 > inject=2 > message=3), per-chat injection dedup, and a per-turn cap on stop+block/replace hooks. Key files: src/hook/{conditions,condition-validator,evaluator,sequence,hook-executor,hook-preprocessor}.ts, src/tools/skill_compile.ts.",
                "level": 3,
                "children": [],
                "links": [
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/hook/conditions.ts",
                    "comment": "ConditionRegistry: manages compiled conditions, lazy compilation pipeline, LLM translation with structured JSON schema, atomic persistence, orphan cleanup, version history, retry loop, intent-language validation for bash actions"
                  },
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/hook/condition-validator.ts",
                    "comment": "Validation pipeline: schema+expression validation via jsep AST walking, dangerous identifier blocking, smoke test against empty mock sequence, compileCondition() orchestrating gates, MockSequence sharing matchesToolSpec semantics"
                  },
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/hook/evaluator.ts",
                    "comment": "AST-based safe evaluator (no eval/Function): preprocesses turn.X(→turnX( for jsep, implements all 8 scoped functions + isPlanMode, string/array methods, binary/logical/unary operators, debug tree printing"
                  },
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/hook/sequence.ts",
                    "comment": "Sequence: turn-scoped (events cleared at markPromptBoundary) vs session-scoped (livelog tallies/pattern/results logs) query API; three-class tool spec matching (plain, skill_load#name, bash#prefix with clause splitting); evaluateWithCall"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "/mycc.md/architecture-core-concepts/grant-system",
                "text": "A permission system for child processes to request approval before performing sensitive operations.\n\n**Intent Language**: The `bash` tool requires an `intent` parameter in the format `VERB OBJECT PARAM PARAM ... TO PURPOSE`, where VERB is one of READ/WRITE/EDIT/DELETE/BUILD/TEST/INSTALL/RUN and OBJECT is one of SOURCE/CONFIG/DEPENDENCY/ARTIFACT/SYSTEM/DATA/TEMP/USER. Example: `READ SOURCE dir=src TO understand dependencies`. Most PARAMs are free-form; a few reserved PARAMs change routing:\n\n- **`dangerous=i_know`** — escape hatch for destructive/irreversible commands (e.g., `rm -rf`, force pushes). Declaring it skips the block AND the LLM safeguard, routing directly to the user `[y/N]` — the human's approval is the real authorization. Only affects `destructive`/`irreversible` categories (the `system` category like `git commit` stays hard-blocked — use `git_commit` instead). Unavailable in child processes. Without it, a blocked command returns a Socratic hint naming the *existence* of a PARAM override but withholding the exact key/value. Example: `DELETE DATA path=build/ dangerous=i_know TO reclaim disk space`.\n- **`batch=i_know`** — skip the LLM safeguard for batch deletions (multiple files/globs/recursive paths). Routes directly to user `[y/N]`. Only affects the DELETE + batch-delete path (does NOT bypass a hard block, does NOT cover `dangerous=i_know` patterns). Unavailable in child processes. Example: `DELETE TEMP batch=i_know TO clean build artifacts` (for `rm -rf dist/ node_modules/`).\n\nImplementation: `src/context/grant/bash-judge.ts`. The full system-prompt wording is emitted by `buildIntentLanguageSection()` in `src/loop/agent-prompts.ts`. The `hand_over` tool requires `intent` as `VERB OBJECT TO PURPOSE`; the correct VERB/OBJECT is `RUN USER TO <purpose>` (running an interactive human session) — any other dimension returns a Socratic hint. `command` is a JSON string (multi-line scripts escape newlines as `\\n`, but prefer one-lining with `&&`/`;`/`||`). It refuses to spawn a nested popup if the agent is itself inside tmux. Grant flow: `Child → requestGrant → IPC → Parent → check mode → check worktree → grant/deny`. In `plan` mode: all code changes blocked. In `normal` mode: auto-grant for owned worktree, reject otherwise.",
                "title": "Grant System",
                "summary": "The Grant System is a permission framework where sensitive operations require approval. Intent Language: `bash` and `hand_over` tools require `intent` in `VERB OBJECT [PARAM...] TO PURPOSE` format (verbs: READ/WRITE/EDIT/DELETE/FIND/BUILD/TEST/INSTALL/RUN; objects: SOURCE/CONFIG/DEPENDENCY/ARTIFACT/SYSTEM/DATA/TEMP/USER). Full wording emitted by `buildIntentLanguageSection()` in `src/loop/agent-prompts.ts`; parsing by `src/context/grant/intent-parser.ts` (single-pass PARAM tokenizer, verb/object vocabularies, read-only vs mutation classification). Implementation: `src/context/grant/bash-judge.ts` runs a 5-step pipeline — dangerous pattern check, missing-intent check, grammar validation, mode+verb check, LLM analysis (parent-only, for RUN/batch-delete) — then user `[y/N]` confirmation if uncertain. Reserved PARAMs: `dangerous=i_know` (skips hard block AND LLM safeguard for destructive/irreversible categories; `system` category like `git commit` stays hard-blocked) and `batch=i_know` (skips batch-delete LLM classifier; no hard block bypass). Both unavailable in child processes. Without `dangerous=i_know`, blocked commands return Socratic hints naming a PARAM override exists without revealing the key. `dangerous-commands.ts` provides category-based patterns with EXEC_WRAPPERS (obfuscation detection) and INDIRECT_WRAPPERS (observation-skip). `grant-evaluator.ts` dispatches: bash → judgeBash; file ops → plan mode blocks (except allowed file + plan-mode-writable dirs), normal mode auto-grants owned worktree. `hand_over` requires `RUN USER` intent — wrong dimensions return Socratic hints naming the wrong dimension but not the answer; rejects leading `tmux` (nesting) and auto mode. Grant flow: `Child → requestGrant → IPC 'grant_request' → Parent → evaluateGrant`.",
                "level": 3,
                "children": [],
                "links": [
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/context/grant/bash-judge.ts",
                    "comment": "Core 5-step bash judging pipeline with dangerous=i_know / batch=i_know escape hatches, Socratic hints, LLM analysis, user confirmation"
                  },
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/context/grant/grant-evaluator.ts",
                    "comment": "Main grant dispatcher: bash → judgeBash, file ops → plan-mode/allowed-file/plan-writable-dir checks; child worktree ownership auto-grant"
                  },
                  {
                    "target_type": "file",
                    "file_path": "src/context/grant/dangerous-commands.ts",
                    "comment": "Pattern list with destructive/irreversible/system categories; INDIRECT_WRAPPERS skip and EXEC_WRAPPERS obfuscation detection"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "links": [
              {
                "target_type": "file",
                "file_path": "src/loop/triologue.ts",
                "comment": "Triologue facade: delegates to triologue/ modules (store, pending-tools, compact, wrap-up, checkpoint, tp-fix)"
              },
              {
                "target_type": "file",
                "file_path": "src/loop/triologue/tp-fix.ts",
                "comment": "TpAutoFixer: TP-violation auto-recovery inside triologue layer; TriologueInternals removed in 2026-08 refactor"
